; 04-no-generic-functions.l0 -- a function works on one list type only, so
; rev and length are rewritten for every element type a program uses.
;
; The builtins cons, head, tail, and is_empty work on any list, but a def
; names its parameter types exactly, so the prelude's rev is only for
; (list int). This file does not check:
;
;   $ tools/l0check lib/prelude.l0 programs/limits/04-no-generic-functions.l0
;   programs/limits/04-no-generic-functions.l0:32:3: error: argument 1 of
;     rev must be (list int), but this is (list Tok)
;
; (on one line). Copies written in these programs, each the same two lines
; with a different type:
;
;   linediff.l0       rev_fronts (list Front), rev_edits (list Edit)
;   linediff_test.l0  length_strs (list (list int))
;   minigrep.l0       rev_toks (list Tok)
;   minigrep_test.l0  length_toks (list Tok)
;
; and the prelude itself has rev_strs_onto for (list (list int)) next to
; rev_onto. Five copies, 10 lines: cheap each time, but the cost recurs in
; every program with a data type, and each copy is a place a typo could
; hide. linediff_test.l0's old_side and new_side are one function written
; twice, differing only in which edits they keep, because there is no
; filter either, which needs function values rather than generics.

(data Tok
  (A)
  (B))

(def backwards ((ts (list Tok))) (list Tok)
  (rev ts))
